  "reasoning_content": "We need to answer user's request: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce detailed technical tutorial. Need maybe final only. Need be careful not repeat. Need likely cover SQL execution: lexer/parser, AST, semantic analysis, catalog, optimization (logical/physical plans, stats, costing), execution (relational algebra operators, access paths, index use, joins, sorts, aggregates), indexing tradeoffs, transactions concurrency isolation MVCC locks, returning rows (cursor, result buffer, network). Concrete examples. Common pitfalls. Need complete sentences. Avoid repeating yourself. Let's plan coherent tutorial.\n\nNeed satisfy \"complete sentences\" maybe avoid bullet fragments? Could use headings, but sentences complete. Avoid repeating. Maybe sections:\n1. Big picture lifecycle\n2. Example query\n3. Parsing\n4. Semantic analysis/catalog\n5. Optimization\n6. Access paths and indexes\n7. Join strategies and examples\n8. Sorting/aggregation/distinct/limits\n9. Transactions and concurrency\n10. Returning rows\n11. Performance pitfalls and mitigation\n12. Worked example with EXPLAIN\n\nNeed detailed. Let's design example:\nSELECT c.city, COUNT(o.id) AS orders, SUM(o.total)\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE c.tier = 'gold' AND o.created_at >= DATE '2024-01-01'\nGROUP BY c.city\nHAVING COUNT(o.id) > 100;\n\nBut join and aggregation. Could include covering index, composite index. Need explain optimizer chooses join order. Example tables: customers(id pk, city, tier, created_at?), orders(id pk, customer_id, total, created_at, status). Indexes: customers(tier, city) and orders(customer_id, created_at, total) covering for join? For HAVING >100, aggregate.\n\nQuery: SELECT c.city, COUNT(*) AS order_count, SUM(o.total) AS revenue FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE c.tier='gold' AND o.created_at >= '2024-01-01' GROUP BY c.city HAVING COUNT(*) > 100 ORDER BY revenue DESC LIMIT ",
